# Deploy targets for scripts/deploy.sh and the /deploy command.
# Copy this file to scripts/deploy.env and fill in your real values.
# scripts/deploy.env is gitignored — never commit real infrastructure values.

# SSH host: an entry in ~/.ssh/config, or user@hostname
DEPLOY_HOST=your-vps

# Absolute path to the app's deploy directory on the server
DEPLOY_PATH=/var/www/psa

# Public domain (no scheme) used for the live URL and health check
DEPLOY_DOMAIN=your-psa-domain

# Branch whose origin ref is deployed when scripts/deploy.sh is called with no
# argument. Optional; defaults to main.
#DEPLOY_BRANCH=main

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SECURITY-RELEVANT. Read this section before copying the file.
#
# scripts/deploy.sh sources this file under `set -a`, ABOVE most of its own
# logic. That makes this file an unreviewed prelude executed as shell code
# inside the reviewed script's scope, not a passive list of values: anything
# assigned here can define or override variables deploy.sh sets later, and
# every assignment is auto-exported. The four keys below are the ones that
# steer the DEPLOY GATE — the control that refuses a deploy whose window
# contains held or un-approved work. They are documented here because a
# selector nobody knows exists cannot be reviewed by anyone.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Absolute path to the review gate executable that deploy.sh consults before
# deploying. Optional, but a deployment that relies on the gate should set it
# explicitly: the built-in default is a path from the original author's own
# machine and will not exist on yours, and a missing gate is a HARD REFUSAL
# (never a skip). deploy.sh prints the resolved path and its sha256 to stdout
# on every run, so a substituted or drifted gate is visible after the fact.
#PSA_DEPLOY_GATE=/absolute/path/to/psa-deploy-gate.sh

# Append-only audit log for the gate identity record and for recorded gate
# overrides. Note it is selected from this same file: relocating the log is
# itself a change to the evidence trail, which is why deploy.sh names the
# resolved path on stdout as well as writing to it.
#PSA_DEPLOY_GATE_AUDIT=/absolute/path/to/psa-deploy-gate.log

# Where those records go if the primary audit log cannot be written. Optional;
# defaults to $TMPDIR/psa-deploy-gate.audit.log. If neither can be written,
# deploy.sh warns loudly rather than dropping the record silently.
#PSA_DEPLOY_GATE_AUDIT_FALLBACK=/absolute/path/to/psa-deploy-gate.audit.log

# 🔴 DO NOT SET THIS HERE. PSA_DEPLOY_GATE_OVERRIDE is the emergency escape
# that lets a deploy proceed when the gate file is MISSING, and it is meant to
# be typed on ONE invocation by a person who is accountable for that run:
#
#     PSA_DEPLOY_GATE_OVERRIDE="<reason>" scripts/deploy.sh <sha>
#
# Because this file is sourced under `set -a`, an assignment here is exported
# and satisfies that check exactly as a typed one does — turning a per-deploy
# decision into a standing one that nobody at the keyboard can see. It is named
# here only so that it is not a selector nobody knows exists; the deploy would
# still be recorded, but "recorded" is not "reviewed".
#PSA_DEPLOY_GATE_OVERRIDE=
